<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nanyue</h1><p class="st-meta">204 BCE – 111 BCE · capital Panyu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom founded by the Qin general Zhao Tuo in Guangdong and northern Vietnam, which blended Chinese and Yue ways and defied the Han for nearly a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> officer sent south in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> lands. As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> collapsed, he sealed the mountain passes, killed officials loyal to Qin and in 204 BCE proclaimed himself King of Nanyue, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Panyu</a> (Guangzhou). He later annexed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/au-lac">Au Lac</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-asia">Red River</a> delta.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a> adopted Yue customs, married into local elites and ruled a mixed population of northern settlers and Yue peoples. He accepted Han overlordship in 196 BCE but styled himself emperor at home. The tomb of his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-mo">Zhao Mo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, with its jade burial suit and seals, shows a court combining Han, Chu and Yue traditions. In Vietnamese history the line is the Trieu dynasty.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 112 BCE the chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-jia">Lü Jia</a> killed the young king and the pro-Han queen dowager. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> sent fleets and armies, and in 111 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Panyu</a> fell and Nanyue was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, beginning a millennium of Chinese rule in northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nanyue.jpg" alt="Nanyue" width="320" height="314"><figcaption>Image: Ssolbergj; derivative work by 长夜无风、玖巧仔 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanyue.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>204 BCE – 111 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Panyu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhao Tuo, Zhao Mo, Zhao Jiande</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion and Yue local cults</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese, Yue languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a>, <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nam Viet, Southern Yue, Nam Việt, Trieu dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-158&amp;focus=nanyue" aria-label="Nanyue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7424 1020 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nanyue lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7768 1187h0M7846 1653h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7744 1276h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7744 1276h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-158&amp;focus=nanyue">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
